CPL target table: the properties files have no intervention.master.hive.table.datavantcpl key, so the original Scala CPL step would fail when it looks it up. I used RAD_DATAVANT_CHASE_CPL, the name given in a code comment.
{{ LOB }} must come through as 'MCR', not '003'. The join filter and EDL_LOB_CD both depend on it.
SUB_GROUP_NAME is set to DATAVANT, which puts the SQL at .../genaisa/sql/DATAVANT/DatavantMbrProvETL.sql. S3 paths are case-sensitive, so check this matches where you deploy it.
Process and step names are DatavantMbrProv Outbound File GenerationMCR and Insert and Outbound File GenerationMCR, with no space before MCR. That's what the source builds by appending the LOB to the property value.
Hardcoded values: DTVNTRT, deploy step 10 and Completed are written into the script. They come from the properties files and are the same in UAT and PROD.
PROD detokenization UDF in UAT: P01_PROTEGRITY...ANTM_SSN_DETOK is hardcoded to the PROD database in the source, and I kept it that way, so UAT also calls it.
Differences from the Scala job
One timestamp folder: both outputs land in the same folder. The Scala job took a separate timestamp for each function, so its two sets of files could end up in different folders.
Run ID: the script uses the framework's EDL_RUN_ID instead of generating md5(current_timestamp) itself.
Load log: writing the load_log_key entry is left to the Python framework, as the template expects.
Email: the failure emails sent by the Scala job are left to the framework's notifications.
CPL empty check: this now runs up front instead of after the member/provider output is written. It uses the same join as the member/provider data, so it can only trigger in the same cases.
Row-count audit: the source's check against the deploy record count is commented out, so stage_count is the deduplicated join count and won't fail on that comparison.
